{
    "test_name": "MOB.535_AssetVerify_Sort_Ordering",
    "details": {
        "name": "MOB.535_AssetVerify_Sort_Ordering",
        "type": "browser",
        "status": "paused",
        "message": "`MOB.535` **The mobile job list really comes out in the chosen order.**\n- Closes `MOB.530`'s long-standing `[~]` \u2014 *sort ordering is not verified* \u2014 which was\n  **blocked on the FIXTURE, not on test effort**: proving an order needs \u22652 records.\n  \u2705 Unblocked 2026-08-23 when the owner confirmed the crew has multiple mobile jobs.\n- \ud83d\udd01 **Version 2. The exact-reversal invariant (`MOB.345`'s shape) does NOT survive\n  virtualisation** \u2014 version 1 failed on its first run, and its own diagnostics said why\n  in one run: ASC and DESC rendered **different row counts and different sets**, because\n  Virtuoso mounts only the visible window.\n- \u2b50 **The invariant is now MONOTONICITY of the rendered window**: `Name \u25b2` \u2192 names\n  non-decreasing, `Name \u25bc` \u2192 non-increasing. Any contiguous window of a sorted list is\n  itself sorted, so this is immune to virtualisation, to how many jobs exist, and to which\n  ones they are \u2014 and it is *stronger* than reversal, because it reads the actual sort key\n  rather than comparing two opaque orders.\n- Sorts by **`name`** because it is the only one of the three offered columns\n  (`createdAt`/`name`/`status`) that the card actually renders \u2014 monotonicity has to read\n  the key off the row.\n- \u26a0\ufe0f Comparison uses `<`/`>` on raw strings to match lodash's `compareAscending`.\n  `localeCompare` disagrees on mixed case and would look like a sort bug.\n- **Cannot go vacuous**: guards require \u22652 rendered rows **and** \u22652 distinct names \u2014\n  monotonicity is trivially true both ways for a list of identical names (trap 5).\n- \ud83d\uded1 **Self-restoring, `alwaysExecute` \u2014 and unlike `MOB.810` it really does clear\n  `mobile-MobileJob-sort`.** Wired **last** in `MOB.996` so a leaked sort cannot reach\n  `MOB.810`, which deliberately leaves one set.\n- \ud83d\uded1 **READ-ONLY**.",
